+# Windows browser supervisor summary
+
+Implemented the Windows browser capture boundary in `spikes/001-auth/src/browser.rs` and `src/browser/native.rs`. Windows now validates an explicit executable override or searches known-folder Edge locations before Chrome locations, launches via `CreateProcessW` suspended, assigns the process to a kill-on-close Job Object before resuming it, and retains an isolated TempDir until the owned process tree is confirmed gone. Capture cancellation now uses Windows console signals while Linux process-group/Wayland behavior remains in its existing branch.
+
+## Verification
+
+- Linux all-target suite passed: 99 library tests, 11 CLI tests, and all example tests.
+- Formatting, Clippy with `-D warnings`, and release build passed.
+- Actual production Windows gate passed: `RUSTFLAGS='-D warnings' cargo check --manifest-path spikes/001-auth/Cargo.toml --lib --bins --target x86_64-pc-windows-msvc`.
+- Windows `--all-targets` check also passed. It reports five pre-existing unused/dead-code warnings in the `credential_size` example; native test source typechecks but was not executed.
+- Focused executable-selection, command-line quoting, launch-order, and cleanup-policy tests passed on Linux.
+
+## Pending
+
+Native Windows execution of Job Object lifecycle tests and Windows signals is pending the Windows boot. Credential Manager/native lock runtime checks remain pending as previously recorded. The Windows helper tests cover child/descendant cleanup and unrelated-process survival in source; failure injection for assignment/resume was not added. No real browser, login, API, or credential operation was performed.
+
+See [windows-browser-evidence.md](windows-browser-evidence.md) for API documentation, lifecycle details, complete command output summary, and SHA-256 snapshots.
