Implemented the Windows browser supervisor in [browser.rs](/home/abdel/Documents/Coding/binus-cli/spikes/001-auth/src/browser.rs) and [native.rs](/home/abdel/Documents/Coding/binus-cli/spikes/001-auth/src/browser/native.rs), and wired it into capture and Windows cancellation. Windows discovery checks Edge before Chrome through known folders; launch uses a suspended process assigned to a kill-on-close Job Object before resume. Cancellation and timeout cleanup wait for the owned tree before removing its isolated profile. Linux process-group and Wayland behavior remains in its platform branch.

The Windows API sequence follows Microsoft’s [CreateProcessW](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/processthreadsapi/nf-processthreadsapi-createprocessw), [process creation flags](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/procthread/process-creation-flags), and [Job Objects](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/procthread/job-objects) documentation.

**Checks passed:** Linux all-target tests (99 library, 11 CLI, plus examples), formatting, Clippy with warnings denied, release build, and the Windows production `--lib --bins` compile with warnings denied. Windows `--tests` also typechecked with warnings denied; the Windows `--all-targets` check passed with five existing warnings in the `credential_size` example.

Native Windows lifecycle tests are written but were not run; Windows boot is still needed to verify Job Object behavior and console signals. No browser, login, API, or credential operation was performed. The focused test results, documentation, and file hashes are in [windows-browser-evidence.md](/home/abdel/Documents/Coding/binus-cli/.agent/windows-browser-evidence.md) and [windows-browser-summary.md](/home/abdel/Documents/Coding/binus-cli/.agent/windows-browser-summary.md).
